  "label": "TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 2",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2020 · Paper 1 · Question 2 — Determine 2p+q from two linear factors of a cubic.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "C",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use the two factors",
      "body": "<p>Set each given factor equal to zero to find its root. Substitute each root into the cubic; the cubic must be zero at both.</p>",
      "recap": "A factor tells you an input where the polynomial is zero.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the coefficient 2 on the cubic term when substituting.",
      "pause": "Write the two equations involving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l02",
          "reminder": "A factor tells you an input where the polynomial is zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Subtract the equations",
      "body": "<p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math> term appears with the same coefficient in both equations. Subtract one equation from the other to remove it.</p>",
      "recap": "Subtracting equations can leave just one unknown.",
      "pitfall": "Subtract every term, including the constant terms.",
      "pause": "Find <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, substitute back for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi></mrow></math>, then calculate the expression in the question.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-simultaneous-equations",
          "reminder": "Subtracting equations can leave just one unknown."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></math>. The factor theorem gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>Subtracting the first equation from the second gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>65</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>15</mn><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>13</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The second equation then gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>; these values also satisfy the first equation.</p><p>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>26</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>22</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, option C.</strong></p>",
